    {
      "id": "2062948861321359773",
      "title": "I completely agree with this. Many Physical AI engineers and researchers tend to underestimate the complexity and challenges of hardware development.\n\nFor examp",
      "body": "I completely agree with this. Many Physical AI engineers and researchers tend to underestimate the complexity and challenges of hardware development.\n\nFor example, YAM lacks several features that are typically required for production deployment of collaborative robots, including a hardware emergency stop (E-stop), force/torque (F/T) sensing, motor brakes, and the ability to operate reliably in 24/7 environments. As a result, it is quite challenging to see how it could currently support safe deployment in production settings.\n\nI think YAM is an excellent platform for research and development, but it does not yet satisfy several key requirements for production-grade robotic deployments.\n\nI'm quite sure the cost of collaborative robots will come down over time as demand increases. However, companies like Fanuc have spent decades refining highly reliable industrial hardware and have been running lights-out manufacturing for more than 20 years. Competing with that level of reliability, quality, and manufacturing expertise is far more challenging than many people appreciate",
      "url": "https://x.com/s1wase/status/2062948861321359773",
      "author": "2605288466",
      "published_at": "2026-06-05T17:25:10",
      "observed_at": "2026-09-10T03:54:55.611861",
